[Music]  each day in the united states over 120  people die from an opioid overdose  many of these deaths are preventable  in fact when someone nearby is  experiencing an overdose your awareness  and action can save their life  but what are opioids how can you  recognize an overdose and how can you  effectively intervene  people have been using opioids for  thousands of years  some of the first opioids opium morphine  and codeine  came from the resin of the opium poppy  more recently a variety of opioid drugs  have been manufactured for medical use  their names may sound familiar  percocet vicodin and fentanyl are a few  our bodies naturally produce opioid-like  substances which bind to receptors in  the brain spinal cord and other parts of  the body resulting in pain relief and  feelings of euphoria  when a person takes opioids they also  bind to these receptors  intensifying these responses  unfortunately using opioids can cause  dependence or addiction where  increasingly large doses are needed to  create the same effects  in large doses opioids can dangerously  depress awareness and breathing and may  lead to permanent injury or death  an opioid overdose usually occurs  accidentally  when the amount of opioids taken  overwhelms the body and sometimes when  opioid effects combine with other  substances that impair awareness and  breathing like alcohol  the likelihood of an overdose does not  discriminate by age gender race or  religion  anyone could suffer from an overdose  even a member of your family or  community  luckily you can help simply by  recognizing the signs of an overdose  it's as easy as abc  a awareness  opioids affect people's awareness by  attaching to receptors in the brain that  keep us alert  a person experiencing an overdose may  appear dazed drowsy or unconscious  you may also notice they have pinpoint  pupils  b  breathing  opioids also act on brain regions  responsible for breathing  an opioid overdose may cause a person to  breathe very slowly  gurgle due to a blockage in the airway  or stop breathing altogether  c  color  the lips and fingertips of someone  experiencing an opioid overdose may be  pale ashen purple or blue because  opioids can deprive the body of oxygen  once you've recognized the signs of an  opioid overdose it's time to act here  are the steps needed to save a life  one assess awareness  talk loudly  shout and give a chest noogie by  pressing your knuckles into the sternum  of the affected individual  to try to get them to respond  2. not responding  then call 9-1-1 and notify those nearby  of the situation  3.  if you're suspecting an overdose  administer naloxone to knock these  opioids off their receptors  this will restore consciousness and  breathing and oxygen can once again  travel throughout the body  if the person is having another medical  emergency such as a stroke or heart  attack naloxone won't hurt them because  it specifically works on opioid  receptors and you've called 9-1-1 so  help for other emergencies is on its way  four restore breathing while waiting for  naloxone to work and help to arrive  open the person's airway and place them  in the recovery position so they can  breathe easier and to prevent them from  choking on their tongue or vomit  if you're comfortable you can give them  rescue breaths  if you know basic life support use those  skills now to determine if chest  compressions and aed are needed  five finally stay with the person until  the ambulance arrives  while you wait for the ambulance you may  have to administer a second dose of  naloxone  you should do this if after a few  minutes the person is still not  responsive or they show signs of their  overdose returning  make sure they get to the hospital for  further observation since overdose may  come back if the naloxone wears off  the keys to saving someone from an  opioid overdose are  first suspected when you see that  awareness breathing and color are  impaired  even without naloxone you can call 9-1-1  ask for help assess awareness and  support breathing  you will be most effective if you carry  naloxone everywhere you go and become  aware of community-placed naloxone in  locations like lobbies or near  defibrillators and fire extinguishers  you can obtain your own naloxone from a  medical provider pharmacist local harm  reduction group or community access  naloxone rescue boxes like nalox boxes  share this video now so others can be  aware and prepared to respond to an  opioid overdose  [Music]  you

